The core principle (important)

A player is not “good”. A player is good for a role, in a context, at a moment in time.

So your AI must rank players using:

Base ability

Role fit

Context modifiers

Reliability

Ceiling (potential)

✅ THE BEST WAY: A 5-LAYER PLAYER EVALUATION MODEL
LAYER 1 — BASE SKILL SCORE (raw ability)

This answers: “How good is this player in a vacuum?”

Example (CS2)
baseSkill =
  rifle * 0.30 +
  awp * 0.25 +
  pistol * 0.10 +
  grenades * 0.10 +
  reaction * 0.15 +
  clutch * 0.10


Rules:

Use weighted averages, not simple means

Weights differ per game and per role

This score is stable over time

📌 This is what casual players expect to see as “overall”

LAYER 2 — ROLE FIT SCORE (this is where realism starts)

This answers: “How good is this player at what they’re supposed to do?”

Example role weights

AWPer

roleFit =
  awp * 0.45 +
  reaction * 0.20 +
  clutch * 0.15 +
  stressResistance * 0.10 +
  positioning * 0.10


IGL

roleFit =
  leader * 0.30 +
  tactic * 0.25 +
  teamwork * 0.20 +
  stressResistance * 0.15 +
  rifle * 0.10


Rules:

A great rifler can be a bad AWPer

Role mismatch should penalize ranking

This prevents “one-stat gods”

📌 This is what separates pros from casual sims

LAYER 3 — CONTEXT MODIFIERS (dynamic)

This answers: “How good is the player right now?”

Modifiers (multiplicative, small)
contextModifier =
  (1 + formBonus) *
  (1 - fatiguePenalty) *
  (1 + moraleBonus) *
  (1 - injuryPenalty)


Example ranges:

Form: ±10%

Morale: ±8%

Fatigue: up to −15%

Injury: −30% or bench

Rules:

Never let modifiers exceed ±25% total

These change weekly

📌 This is what makes form slumps and hot streaks real

LAYER 4 — RELIABILITY / CONSISTENCY

This answers: “Can I trust this player?”

Key stats:

Stress resistance

Productivity

Past variance in performance

Example:

reliability =
  (stressResistance * 0.5 + productivity * 0.5) / 100


Usage:

finalScore *= (0.85 + reliability * 0.15)


Rules:

Low reliability = boom/bust players

High reliability = boring but valuable

📌 This is how “consistent veterans” stay relevant

LAYER 5 — POTENTIAL & AGE (future value)

This answers: “Is this player worth investing in?”

Used for:

Transfers

Scouting

AI roster planning

Example:

futureValue =
  finalScore *
  (1 + (potential - currentStatsAvg) * ageFactor)


Rules:

Young + high potential boosts value

Old + capped stats reduces long-term rank

Should NOT affect match-day strength

📌 This is why teams sign young players

🧠 FINAL RANKING FORMULA (CLEAN)
matchDayRating =
  baseSkill *
  roleFitMultiplier *
  contextModifier *
  reliabilityFactor

transferRanking =
  matchDayRating *
  futureValueFactor

globalReputation =
  rollingAverage(matchDayRating over last N matches)

📊 HOW TO PRESENT THIS IN UI (IMPORTANT)
NEVER show the full formula

Instead show:

Overall Rating (rounded)

Role Rating (AWPer ★★★★☆)

Form indicator (↑ ↓)

Consistency tag (“Consistent”, “Streaky”)

Internally: math
Externally: intuition

❌ WHAT NOT TO DO

These kill realism fast:

❌ One “overall” stat

❌ Flat averages

❌ Ignoring role mismatch

❌ Ignoring fatigue/morale

❌ Potential affecting match strength

❌ Random “hidden boosts”

🧩 OPTIONAL (ADVANCED, BUT WORTH IT)

If you want elite-level depth later:

Separate mechanical skill vs decision-making

Map-specific performance memory

Team synergy multipliers (pairwise chemistry)

Opponent matchup modifiers

TL;DR (what to implement first)

If you only implement ONE thing correctly:

Separate Base Skill, Role Fit, and Context Modifiers

That alone will make your AI feel smart.